B-0 (Boosty) -- pthread_getattr_np() tells the truth about the main thread's stack.

Upstream musl has no record of the initial thread's stack extent, so its
pthread_getattr_np() arm for that thread (t->stack == 0) DISCOVERS it: start
one page below the page-rounded auxv address and walk downward with

    mremap(p-l-PAGE_SIZE, PAGE_SIZE, 2*PAGE_SIZE, 0)

until the call stops failing with ENOMEM. The 0001 seam parks __NR_mremap at
the 0xFFFF ENOSYS sentinel (0003: pouch has no mremap), so the very first test
fails with ENOSYS, not ENOMEM, the loop body never runs, and the function
reports stacksize = PAGE_SIZE.

Measured on the device (2026-09-21, a static probe, HVF):

    main   base=0x7ffff000  size=4096    sp=0x7ffffdb8
    thread base=0x100007000 size=134920  (correct: musl's own 128 KiB default)

The real main stack is [0x7ff00000, 0x80000000): 1 MiB, committed whole at
exec, over a one-page guard (kernel/include/thylacine/exec.h,
EXEC_USER_STACK_TOP / EXEC_USER_STACK_SIZE). Nothing in the tree asked until
JavaScriptCore did: WTF::StackBounds feeds the VM's recursion limit from these
bounds, so jsc threw a stack overflow on its first call and could not even
print it (exit 3, no output). The same wrong answer would reach any
conservative collector and Rust std's main-thread stack guard.

The fix states the mapping the kernel actually makes. The two constants MIRROR
exec.h; the device-side pin is pouch-hello-threads, which now asserts that a
main-thread local lies inside the reported bounds and that the bounds are the
full mapping -- so a change to the kernel's stack layout fails a boot prover
instead of silently making libc wrong again.

PAIRED WITH (same commit, not in this patch file): usr/pouch-hello/
pouch-hello-threads.c gains the main-stack check.

Not addressed here, recorded for the platform-tranche conversation: 1 MiB is
small for a JavaScript engine's main thread (JavaScriptCore asks for 5 MiB and
clamps to what it is given). exec.h already names the answer -- a large lazy
reservation -- as its tracked next lift.

--- a/src/thread/pthread_getattr_np.c
+++ b/src/thread/pthread_getattr_np.c
@@ -3,6 +3,9 @@
 #include "libc.h"
 #include <sys/mman.h>
 
+#define POUCH_MAIN_STACK_TOP  0x0000000080000000ul
+#define POUCH_MAIN_STACK_SIZE (1024ul * 1024ul)
+
 int pthread_getattr_np(pthread_t t, pthread_attr_t *a)
 {
 	*a = (pthread_attr_t){0};
@@ -12,13 +15,19 @@
 		a->_a_stackaddr = (uintptr_t)t->stack;
 		a->_a_stacksize = t->stack_size;
 	} else {
-		char *p = (void *)libc.auxv;
-		size_t l = PAGE_SIZE;
-		p += -(uintptr_t)p & PAGE_SIZE-1;
-		a->_a_stackaddr = (uintptr_t)p;
-		while (mremap(p-l-PAGE_SIZE, PAGE_SIZE, 2*PAGE_SIZE, 0)==MAP_FAILED && errno==ENOMEM)
-			l += PAGE_SIZE;
-		a->_a_stacksize = l;
+		/* Thylacine: the initial thread's stack is the fixed exec
+		 * mapping [TOP - SIZE, TOP), committed whole at exec above a
+		 * one-page guard (kernel exec.h: EXEC_USER_STACK_TOP / _SIZE;
+		 * mirrored here, and pinned from the device side by
+		 * pouch-hello-threads' main-stack check). Upstream finds the
+		 * extent by probing downward with mremap(), which the seam
+		 * ENOSYSes -- so the loop ended on its first test and every
+		 * pouch program was told its main stack is ONE PAGE. A runtime
+		 * that sizes recursion or scans its stack from these bounds
+		 * (JavaScriptCore, a conservative GC, Rust std's main-thread
+		 * guard) then fails on its first call. */
+		a->_a_stackaddr = POUCH_MAIN_STACK_TOP;
+		a->_a_stacksize = POUCH_MAIN_STACK_SIZE;
 	}
 	return 0;
 }
